## What a cure actually consists of

A respiratory cure is not a single treatment but a composed daily routine. What goes into it is decided by the spa physician at the entry examination, on the basis of diagnosis, age and any accompanying conditions — so what follows is a map of the possibilities, not a prescription.

- **Mineral water inhalation.** The foundation. Finely atomised Forest Spring water moistens the mucous membranes, helps loosen secretions and makes coughing them up easier. Two sessions a day are typical.
- **Breathing rehabilitation.** Individual and group physiotherapy focused on breathing patterns: prolonged exhalation, diaphragmatic breathing, coughing technique. This is the part of the cure you take home permanently.
- **Carbon dioxide baths and dry gas applications.** Natural CO₂ improves microcirculation and the supply of oxygen to the tissues; in Mariánské Lázně it comes from a local source.
- **Drinking cure.** Supplementary, according to diagnosis. One rule admits no exceptions: which spring, how much and when is always set individually by a doctor. Our [drinking cure guide](/en/magazine/drinking-cure-guide) explains how that works in practice.
- **Climate therapy and terrain cure.** Guided walks at a defined pace and gradient — precisely what the historic network of promenade paths was built for.
- **Supporting treatments.** Depending on your condition, these may include massage, a salt cave (there is one at the Hvězda hotel) or relaxation techniques that help with breathlessness and tension.

## Who the treatment is for

Czech spa medicine works from an indication list forming part of Decree No. 2/2015 Coll. on the professional criteria for spa rehabilitation care (most recently amended by Decree No. 586/2025 Coll.). Respiratory disorders make up indication group V.

For adults, the Mariánské Lázně medical spa company lists in particular:

- chronic inflammations of the airways
- bronchial asthma
- allergic rhinitis
- conditions following surgery of the lower respiratory tract
- COPD and states after pneumonia, including the after-effects of COVID-19

Exactly which diagnoses qualify, the admission conditions, the length of stay and whether a cure can be repeated are all governed by the current wording of the indication list, and the referral is issued by the treating physician. That route applies to patients within the Czech public system; international guests usually come as self-payers, receiving the same treatments without the referral procedure and able to book shorter preventive or convalescent stays. If your aim after a serious respiratory illness is chiefly to rebuild strength, see our article on [post-COVID rehabilitation](/en/magazine/post-covid-rehabilitation).

There are contraindications too — acute infectious illness, active malignancy, pregnancy or an inability to manage daily self-care. That assessment belongs with a doctor, not a search engine.

## Why autumn in particular

An autumn date has concrete advantages for the airways.

**The pollen recedes.** The grass season ends in summer, but mugwort and ragweed flower into October. It is October when airborne pollen drops away markedly — so a patient with an allergic component can take the cure at a moment when their mucous membranes are not fighting an allergen at the same time. If mugwort affects you, discuss the timing with your doctor; for some, the second half of October is ideal.

**The heating season begins.** Dry air in overheated flats and offices is one of the year's least forgiving combinations for the upper airways. Two or three weeks in cool, moist air, inhaling twice a day, is good preparation for winter.

**The town settles.** After August the festival crowds leave and the spa returns to the pace people come here for: no queues at the springs, room on the promenade, treatments easier to schedule.

**The forest is at its best.** Damp, cool, resinous air after autumn rain is ideal for breathing exercises outdoors — and the beech and maple stands above the colonnade turn an ordinary terrain cure into something worth looking at.

## Length of stay, and what to expect

A therapeutic stay usually runs two to three weeks; anything shorter works as prevention or convalescence rather than treatment. In the experience of spa physicians here, the first subjective relief — easier expectoration, a clearer nose, calmer breathing at night — tends to arrive after four or five days, once the body has settled into the rhythm and the inhalations begin to accumulate.

Keep expectations realistic. A cure does not replace prescribed medication, and it will not cure a chronic condition. Its aims are to calm the mucous membranes, open the airways, train a workable breathing technique, and send you home in better condition and needing less rescue medication. With chronic diagnoses the benefit is counted in months, which is why the cure is repeated for many indications.

## An autumn diary: what is on in town

Treatments take up the morning; the afternoon is yours. The autumn programme is busier than you might expect:

- **17–20 September 2026** — the Marienbad Autumn international folklore festival, with ensembles from Italy, Poland and Slovakia; opening at the Chopin House, gala concert at the Municipal Theatre.
- **7–20 September 2026** — European Heritage Days, this year on the theme of endangered monuments, opening buildings that are closed for the rest of the year.
- **September** — the traditional mushroom exhibition drawn from the spa forests, and a food festival on the colonnade.
- **October** — the spa apple festival in the town centre.
- **until 31 October** — the Singing Fountain plays on every odd hour from 7 a.m., with illuminated evening performances at 9 and 10 p.m. Then it falls silent until spring.

Colonnade concerts by the West Bohemian Symphony Orchestra run right through the season — the pleasantest way to spend an hour outdoors without walking a single kilometre.

## Before you pack

Bring layers: mornings in the valley are misty and cold, afternoons can be fifteen degrees and sunny. Add a waterproof jacket and shoes that cope with forest paths after rain, and a flask, because drinking after inhalations is part of the routine. Above all, bring recent reports from your GP or specialist — without them the spa physician cannot build a treatment plan that genuinely matches your condition.

Mariánské Lázně in autumn offers something no other season provides: quiet, clean air and time. For the respiratory tract, that is a combination no pharmacy stocks.
